{"title":"All Paths From Source To Target","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"Given a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of n nodes labeled from 0 to n - 1, find all possible paths from node 0 to node n - 1. Return the paths in lexicographic order, comparing node labels from left to right.\nThe graph is given as follows: graph[i] is a list of all nodes you can visit from node i (i.e., there is a directed edge from node i to node graph[i][j]).\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1:\n\n```text\nInput: graph = [[1,2],[3],[3],[]]\nOutput: [[0,1,3],[0,2,3]]\n```\nExplanation: There are two paths: 0 -> 1 -> 3 and 0 -> 2 -> 3.\n\n### Example 2:\n\n```text\nInput: graph = [[4,3,1],[3,2,4],[3],[4],[]]\nOutput: [[0,1,2,3,4],[0,1,3,4],[0,1,4],[0,3,4],[0,4]]\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- n == graph.length\n- 2 <= n <= 15\n- 0 <= graph[i][j] < n\n- graph[i][j] != i (i.e., there will be no self-loops).\n- All the elements of graph[i] are unique.\n- The input graph is guaranteed to be a DAG.\n","starterCode":"class Solution:\n    def allPathsSourceTarget(self, graph: List[List[int]]) -> List[List[int]]:\n        pass\n","entryPoint":"Solution().allPathsSourceTarget","parameters":[{"name":"graph","type":"List[List[int]]"}],"returnType":"List[List[int]]","tests":[{"input":[[[1,2,3,6],[3],[3,6],[5,8],[6],[6],[],[],[]]],"expected":[[0,1,3,8],[0,2,3,8],[0,3,8]]},{"input":[[[1,2,3],[2,3],[4],[4],[]]],"expected":[[0,1,2,4],[0,1,3,4],[0,2,4],[0,3,4]]},{"input":[[[1,2,3],[2,5],[],[],[],[]]],"expected":[[0,1,5]]},{"input":[[[1,2,3],[2],[],[]]],"expected":[[0,3]]},{"input":[[[1,2,3],[],[7],[],[],[],[7],[]]],"expected":[[0,2,7]]},{"input":[[[1,2,3],[],[],[]]],"expected":[[0,3]]},{"input":[[[1,2,4,5],[2,5],[5],[],[],[]]],"expected":[[0,1,2,5],[0,1,5],[0,2,5],[0,5]]},{"input":[[[1,2,4,5],[3],[],[6],[6],[7],[],[]]],"expected":[[0,5,7]]},{"input":[[[1,2,4,6],[4,7],[],[7],[],[],[7],[]]],"expected":[[0,1,7],[0,6,7]]},{"input":[[[1,2,4],[2],[3,4,6],[],[5],[],[]]],"expected":[[0,1,2,6],[0,2,6]]},{"input":[[[1,2,4]